","k":"mobile_phone","la":19.649662,"lo":-156.00485,"d":1352,"w":"https://www.t-mobile.com/stores/bd/t-mobile-kona-hi-96740-364"},{"n":"Verizon","g":"essentials","k":"mobile_phone","la":19.650855,"lo":-156.003923,"d":1408},{"n":"Island Naturals Market and Deli","g":"essentials","k":"supermarket","la":19.651089,"lo":-156.003716,"d":1419,"h":"Mo-Fr 08:00-18:00, Sa-Su 09:00-17:00","w":"https://islandnaturals.com","ph":"+1 808-326-1122"},{"n":"Kona Town Market","g":"essentials","k":"supermarket","la":19.628867,"lo":-155.985437,"d":1731},{"n":"Kamakahonu","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":19.639006,"lo":-155.997586,"d":126,"ds":"Kamakahonu, the residence of Kamehameha I, was located at the north end of Kailua Bay in Kailua-Kona on Hawaiʻi Island.","wpu":"Kamakahonu","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/15/Kamakahonu%2C_Kona.jpg/960px-Kamakahonu%2C_Kona.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Huliheʻe Palace","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":19.639444,"lo":-155.994444,"d":274,"ds":"The Huliheʻe Palace is located in historic Kailua-Kona, Hawaiʻi, on Ali'i Drive. The former vacation home of Hawaiian royalty, it was converted to a museum run by the Daughters of Hawaiʻi, showcasing furniture and artifacts. It is located at 75–5718 Aliʻi Drive, Kailua-Kona.","wpu":"Huliheʻe Palace","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/34/Backside_of_the_Hulihee_Palace.JPG/960px-Backside_of_the_Hulihee_Palace.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"St. Michael the Archangel Church (Kailua-Kona, Hawaii)","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":19.636944,"lo":-155.991111,"d":704,"ds":"Saint Michael the Archangel Catholic Church is a parish of the Roman Catholic Church of Hawaiʻi in the United States. Located in Kailua-Kona on the Big Island of Hawaiʻi, 75-5769 Ali'i Drive, coordinates 19°38′13″N 155°59′28″W. The church falls under the jurisdiction of the Diocese of Honolulu and its bishop. On June 17, 1839, Kamehameha III declared religious freedom in the Kingdom in the Edict of Toleration.","wpu":"St. Michael the Archangel Church (Kailua-Kona, Hawaii)","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c5/St_Michael_the_Archangel_Church%2C_Kailua-Kona%2C_Hawaii.jpg/960px-St_Michael_the_Archangel_Church%2C_Kailua-Kona%2C_Hawaii.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Kona Hawaii Temple","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":19.641611,"lo":-155.985528,"d":1214,"ds":"The Kona Hawaii Temple is the 70th operating temple of the Church of Jesus Christ of Latter-day Saints (LDS Church). The intent to build the temple was announced on May 7, 1998, by the church's First Presidency. It is located in Kailua-Kona on the island of Hawaii and is the second temple built in Hawaii, along with the Laie Hawaii Temple, and is the sixth in the Pacific Islands.","wpu":"Kona Hawaii Temple","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8d/Kona_Hawaii_Temple.jpg/960px-Kona_Hawaii_Temple.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Honokōhau Harbor","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":19.669,"lo":-156.024,"d":4288,"ds":"Honokōhau Harbor, officially named Honokōhau Small Boat Harbor, is a marina in Kailua Kona, Hawaii, United States It was built during the 1960s on the lava seashore of western Hawaii Island.","wpu":"Honokōhau Harbor","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/40/Honokohau_Harbor-Marina.jpg/960px-Honokohau_Harbor-Marina.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Hōlualoa Bay","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":19.602161,"lo":-155.974833,"d":4805,"ds":"Hōlualoa Bay is a historic area between Kailua-Kona and Keauhou Bay in the Kona District of the Big Island of Hawaiʻi. The community now called Hōlualoa is uphill (mauka in the Hawaiian Language) from this bay.","wpu":"Hōlualoa Bay","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c6/Kamoa_Point%2C_Kona.jpg/960px-Kamoa_Point%2C_Kona.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Kaloko-Honokōhau National Historical Park","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":19.6787,"lo":-156.022,"d":5036,"ds":"Kaloko-Honokōhau National Historical Park is a United States National Historical Park located in the Kona District on Hawaiʻi Island in the U.S. state of Hawaiʻi. It includes the National Historic Landmarked archaeological site known as the Honokōhau Settlement. The park was established on November 10, 1978, for the preservation, protection and interpretation of traditional native Hawaiian activities and culture.","wpu":"Kaloko-Honokōhau National Historical Park","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/36/Honokohau_Halau.jpg/960px-Honokohau_Halau.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Laʻaloa Bay","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":19.593889,"lo":-155.971389,"d":5786,"ds":"Laʻaloa Bay is a popular recreation area in Kailua-Kona, on the Big Island of Hawaiʻi. Also known as \"Magic Sands\" or \"White Sands Beach\", the official name is \"Laʻaloa Beach County Park\". During calm weather, it is one of the only fine white sandy beaches in the Kailua-Kona area.","wpu":"Laʻaloa Bay","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8f/Magic_Sands_%28Laaloa_Bay%29_Hawaii_Christmas.jpg/960px-Magic_Sands_%28Laaloa_Bay%29_Hawaii_Christmas.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Kahaluʻu Bay","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":19.580278,"lo":-155.967417,"d":7328,"ds":"Kahaluʻu Bay (; Hawaiian pronunciation: [ˈkɐhɐˈluʔu]) is a historic district and popular recreation area on the Kona coast of the Big Island of Hawaiʻi.","wpu":"Kahaluʻu Bay","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/38/Kahaluu_Bay.jpg/960px-Kahaluu_Bay.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Keauhou, Hawaii","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":19.565278,"lo":-155.965,"d":8959,"ds":"Keauhou is an unincorporated community on the island of Hawaii in Hawaii County, Hawaii, United States. Its elevation is 13 feet (4 m). Because the community has borne multiple names, the Board on Geographic Names officially designated it \"Keauhou\" in 1914.","wpu":"Keauhou, Hawaii","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9f/Museum_at_Keauhou.jpg/960px-Museum_at_Keauhou.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Natural Energy Laboratory of Hawaii Authority","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":19.716111,"lo":-156.033611,"d":9290,"ds":"The Natural Energy Laboratory of Hawaii Authority (NELHA) administers the Hawaii Ocean Science and Technology Park (HOST Park) in the U.S. state of Hawaii. NELHA was founded in 1974. At 870 acres (350 ha), HOST Park is a state-subsidized industrial park for incubator and marginal commercial ventures.","wpu":"Natural Energy Laboratory of Hawaii Authority","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a9/NELHA_1.jpg/500px-NELHA_1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Kāneaka Hōlua Slide","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":19.562342,"lo":-155.958472,"d":9531,"ds":"The Kāneaka Hōlua Slide, a.k.a. the Keauhou Hōlua Slide, is located in Keauhou (originally Kahaluʻu) on the island of Hawaiʻi. It is the largest historical hōlua course left in the islands.","wpu":"Kāneaka Hōlua Slide","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/51/Keauhou_Holua.jpg/960px-Keauhou_Holua.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Keauhou Bay","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":19.560794,"lo":-155.962014,"d":9539,"ds":"Keauhou Bay is a historic area in the Kona District of the Big Island of Hawaiʻi. The name comes from ke au hou which means \"the new era\" in the Hawaiian Language.","wpu":"Keauhou Bay","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2f/Kamehameha_III%27s_Birthplace.jpg/960px-Kamehameha_III%27s_Birthplace.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"}],"about":{"ds":"Hualālai is an active shield volcano on the island of Hawaiʻi in the Hawaiian Islands. It is the westernmost, third-youngest and the third-most active of the five volcanoes that form the island of Hawaiʻi, following Kīlauea and the much larger Mauna Loa. Its peak stands 8,271 feet (2,521 m) above sea level.","wpu":"Hualālai","img":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/3/31/Hual%C4%81lai_1996.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled"},"wk":"2026-10-18"}